{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013pmEYRSuiAia4hTG6ZYtsM","type":"tool_result","content":"concerns the computational complexity of the MOSP: is MOSP NP-hard?\n\f           A. Linhares, H.H. Yanasse / Computers & Operations Research 29 (2002) 1759–1772         1761\n\n2. Computational complexity of the MOSP\n\nProposition 1. MOSP is NP-hard.\n\nProof. Reduction of modi:ed cutwidth to MOSP.\n\n  Modi:ed cutwidth (MCUT) [3]\n  Instance: Graph G = (V; E), positive integer K.\n  Question: Is there a one-to-one function : V → {1; 2; : : : ; |V |} such that for all i; 1 ¡ i ¡ |V |,\n      |{(u; v) ∈ E: (u) ¡ i ¡ (v)}| 6 K?\n\n   This problem asks for a linear ordering of the vertices of G on a straight line such that\nno orthogonal plane that intersects the line will cross more than K edges of G. (It diNers\nfrom the minimum cut linear arrangement problem, which is also known as cutwidth, by\nthe use of “¡” sign instead of a “6”.) The optimization version of MCUT asks to mini-\nmize K over all possible linear orderings of the vertices of G, i.e., min ∈\u0010 ZMCUT (G), where\nZMCUT (G) = max1¡i¡|V | |{(u; v) ∈ E: (u) ¡ i ¡ (v)}|.\n   We will show how to solve an instance G = (V; E) of MCUT with an algorithm for MOSP.\nFirst, construct a corresponding I × J matrix P where I = |E | and J = |V | where we associate\neach edge in E to a row number and each vertex of V to a column number. Consider an\nedge (u; v) ∈ E; u ∈ V; v ∈ V . Let i be the row number assigned to this edge and let j and k be\nthe number of the columns assigned to vertices u and v respectively. Make pij = pik = 1, and\npil = 0 for all l \b= j; l \b= k.\n\nLemma 1. Each edge crossing in a linear layout of the vertices of G corresponds to one and\nonly one “:ll-in” to problem matrix Q ; associated to a permutation of the columns of P with\nthe consecutive-ones property for columns.\n\nProof. Consider an ordering of the vertices of G and an edge (u; v) crossing an orthogonal\nplane at vertex i. Let x be the row in matrix P associated with this edge. From the construction\nof P, we have px; (u) = px; (v) = 1, and px; y = 0 for all y ¡ (u) and for all y ¿ (v). At this\nrow, following the column sequence that corresponds to the vertex ordering, there must be a\n:ll-in at position (row x, column i), since the associated matrix Q holds the consecutive-ones\nproperty for columns under permutation .\n\n   Now that this basic relation is established, all that must be done is to ensure that the maximum\ncolumn sum of the MOSP re*ects only the :ll-ins. In order to achieve this, we create a matrix\nP \u0005 as follows:\n       pij\u0005 = pij ; ∀i ∈ {1; 2; : : : ; I }; ∀j ∈ {1; 2; : : : ; J }:\n                \u0003                                                    \u0003 \u0003\nLet C = maxj Ii=1 pij , and compute a gap g = C:J − Jj=1 Ii=1 pij . The rows I +1; I +2; : : : ; I +g\nof P \u0005 have only a single nonzero element equal to 1. They are distributed  \u0003 over the columns\nin rows I + 1; I + 2; : : : ; I + g in such a way that there are exactly C − Ii=1 pij ones on each\n\f1762       A. Linhares, H.H. Yanasse / Computers & Operations Research 29 (2002) 1759–1772\n\n\n\n\n                 Fig. 1. A set of gates and transistors of a gate matrix layout style circuit.\n\n\ncolumn so that columns of P \u0005 have column sum equal to C. Now, for the corresponding MOSP\ninstance P \u0005 [of dimension (I + g) × J ], we have:\n\nLemma 2. ZMOSP (P \u0005 ) = ZMCUT (G) + C.\n\nProof. Observe that each column of P \u0005 has the same number of 1’s. Thus, when solving MOSP\nwhat is being minimized is the maximum column sum of :ll-ins. By Lemma 1, the solution to\nthis instance of MOSP solves the corresponding instance of MCUT.\n\n  Proposition 1 closes the conjecture posed earlier [1,4].\n\n2.1. Relations to VLSI design\n\n   One basic characteristic of the MOSP is the consecutive-ones property: a stack is open at the\nmoment that the :rst piece of each type is cut, and only closed when the last piece of that type\nis cut. Many combinatorial optimization problems also exhibit the consecutive-ones property. A\nproblem that is intrinsically related to the MOSP arises on VLSI design, and is known as the\ngate matrix layout problem (GMLP).\n   A gate matrix layout circuit consists of a set of gates (vertical wires) with transistors (dots)\nthat are used to interconnect the gates. In Fig. 1 such a set of gates before their interconnection\nis shown. There must be horizontal wires known as nets interconnecting all the gates that share\ntransistors at the same position. If we model the problem as a matrix problem, we are given\na binary I × J matrix P = {pij }, with pij = 1 if gate j holds a transistor at position i, and\npij = 0 otherwise, then, the implementation of the nets leads to the consecutive-ones property.\nThe wiring between gates is represented by the ones interconnecting the leftmost and rightmost\ngates, hence, the resultant matrix holds the consecutive-ones property (therefore, we can use\nthe associated matrix Q de:ned previously).\n   One basic feature of the problem is that the sequencing of the gates does not alter the\nunderlying logic equation implemented by the circuit. Furthermore, it is essential to sequence\nthe gates in order to minimize the number of tracks, i.e., the number of necessary physical rows\nto implement the circuit, given by\n                                    I\n                                    \u0002\n       ZGMLP (P) = max                    qij :                                                  (3)\n                  j∈{1; ::: ; J }\n                                    i=1\n\nTherefore, GMLP is the problem of min ∈\u0010 ZGMLP (P).\n\f           A. Linhares, H.H. Yanasse / Computers & Operations Research 29 (2002) 1759–1772   1763\n\n\n\n\n                     Fig. 2. Illustration of the gate matrix layout problem (GMLP).\n\n\n\n   The number of tracks is the only variable determining the overall circuit area, since the\nnumber of gates is constant. In Fig. 2 the gate numbering and number of transistors (plus\ninterconnections) are shown above and below the gates, respectively. In Figs. 2(a) and (b)\ndistinct gate permutations are displayed, with the corresponding variation in each column sum.\nSince the permutation displayed in Fig. 2(b) yields a maximum column sum of 4, it is possible\nto pack the layout in 4 tracks (physical rows), as shown in Fig. 2(c).\n   We thus have\n\n\nProposition 2. For any matrix P; the number of open stacks ZMOSP (P) equals the number of\ntracks ZGMLP (P).\n\nProof. Follows trivially from their de:nitions.\n\n   Proposition 2 shows that the computation of MOSP and GMLP is equivalent. Proposition 2\nis also another proof of the NP-hardness of MOSP, since GMLP is NP-hard [5 –8].\n   A skeptical reader may argue that “there is no need for Proposition 1, as Proposition 2\nalone would do the job of proving NP-hardness of MOSP”. That is true. However, there is\nan additional issue here. We are not only interested in the theoretical issues of computational\ncomplexity, but also in the practical issues of comprehending what kind of problem can actually\nbe solved by an exact MOSP algorithm (such as that proposed in Yanasse [1]). Consider the\nfollowing facts: The NP-hardness of GMLP has been proved by a reduction from the interval\ngraph augmentation problem (IGAP) [5]. However, in practice, an exact algorithm for the GMLP\n(and hence MOSP) cannot be of use to solve IGAP because the number of columns of the\nGMLP matrix grows very rapidly on the size of the IGAP graph—the growth is in fact O(n2 )\nbecause each column represents an edge of the graph [6]. On the other hand, the number of\ncolumns grows only linearly on the modi:ed cutwidth problem, because each column represents\na vertex of the graph. Since the number of possible solutions depends crucially on the number\nof columns (which are the obje