{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WK4aEehfkJ8oEPPgB1dAhF","type":"tool_result","content":"these problems generally is to :nd a particular sequence of production patterns that minimizes production\ncosts. An important pattern-sequencing problem arising in settings as distinct as in the sequencing of\ncutting stock and in factories employing *exible machines is known as the minimization of open stacks\nproblem (MOSP). Though this problem has been recently analyzed in detail, its computational complexity\nremained an open question, as were some important conjectures relating it to other industrial problems,\nsuch as the minimization of order spread problem (MORP), the minimization of tool switches problem\n(MTSP), and the minimization of discontinuities problem (MDP). We deal with these questions, and,\nmoreover, we show that the MOSP is deeply related to a problem in the :eld of VLSI layout, thus\nenabling a link between researchers in computer science and operations research. ? 2002 Published by\nElsevier Science Ltd.\n\nKeywords: Pattern sequencing; Flexible machines; VLSI layout; Computational complexity\n\n\n\n    ∗\n    Corresponding author. Brazilian School of Public Administration, EBAP=FGV, Praia Botafogo 190=426, Rio de\nJaneiro 22257-970, Brazil.\n  E-mail addresses: linhares@fgv.br (A. Linhares), horacio@lac.inpe.br (H.H. Yanasse).\n\n0305-0548/02/$ - see front matter ? 2002 Published by Elsevier Science Ltd.\nPII: S 0 3 0 5 - 0 5 4 8 ( 0 1 ) 0 0 0 5 4 - 5\n\f1760           A. Linhares, H.H. Yanasse / Computers & Operations Research 29 (2002) 1759–1772\n\n1. Introduction\n\n   Consider a production setting where J distinct patterns need to be cut. Each one of these\npatterns may contain a combination of at most I piece types. We can de:ne the piece-pattern\nrelationship by a I × J binary matrix P = {pij }, with pij = 1 if pattern j contains piece type i,\nand pij = 0 otherwise.\n   When a pattern is cut, the pieces are stored on stacks that remain :xed around the cutting\nsaw until each stack is completed. Each stack holds only pieces of the same type and it re-\nmains open until the last pattern containing that piece type is cut. DiIculties in handling a\nlarger number of distinct open stacks appear, for instance, if the number increases beyond the\nsystem capability, so some of the stacks must be temporarily removed, yielding additional costs,\nhigher production time, and higher associated risks (as observed in glass-cutting settings). We\nare thus interested in the sequencing of the patterns to minimize the maximum number of open\nstacks during the cutting process (An analogous problem arises from the sequencing of tasks in\na *exible machine with tooling constraints [1]). An open stacks versus cutting instants matrix\nQ = {qij } is de:ned by\n               \u0001\n                   1; if ∃x; ∃y | (x) 6 j 6 (y) and       pix = piy = 1;\n       qij =                                                                                     (1)\n                   0; otherwise;\n\nwhere denotes a permutation of the {1; 2; : : : ; J } numbers, and de:nes a sequence on which\nthe patterns are cut, such that (i) is the order (instant) in which the ith pattern is cut. Note\nthat matrix Q holds the consecutive-ones property for columns [2] under permutation : in\neach row, any zero between two ones will be changed to one. This is called a :ll-in. Since we\nare interested in minimizing the number of simultaneous open stacks, we de:ne the following\ncost functional:\n\n                                         I\n                                         \u0002\n       ZMOSP (P) = max                         qij                                               (2)\n                       j∈{1; ::: ; J }\n                                         i=1\n\n\nand de:ne the minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) as the problem of min ∈\u0010 ZMOSP (P),\nwhere \u0010 is the set of all possible one-to-one mappings : {1; 2; : : : ; J } → {1; 2; : : : ; J }.\n   In Yanasse [1], a nontrivial mathematical formulation is presented, a branch and bound scheme\nto solve it is proposed, and a number of conjectures are raised. In this work we deal with those\nconjectures. Section 2 deals with the computational complexity of the MOSP, and the relations\nto problems such as modi:ed cutwidth and problems in VLSI design. Section 3 considers\nsome related pattern-sequencing problems, focusing on the possibility of solving a speci:c\nproblem by approaching a related one. Section 4 places a new conjecture, based on a previously\nunknown relation between the minimization of tool switches problem and the minimization of\ndiscontinuities problem, which will be de:ned below. The conclusion discusses how our results\nin*uence the development of real applications. The :rst conjecture that should be dealt with\nconcerns the computational complexity of the MOSP: is MOSP NP-hard?","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9ade16e4-7c1a-4abd-aec7-7c767f55a728","uuid":"f6609044-8307-47ef-adb5-97d52bed5ab8","timestamp":"2026-09-30T10:47:29.589Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"these problems generally is to :nd a particular sequence of production patterns that minimizes production\ncosts. An important pattern-sequencing problem arising in settings as distinct as in the sequencing of\ncutting stock and in factories employing *exible machines is known as the minimization of open stacks\nproblem (MOSP). Though this problem has been recently analyzed in detail, its computational complexity\nremained an open question, as were some important conjectures relating it to other industrial problems,\nsuch as the minimization of order spread problem (MORP), the minimization of tool switches problem\n(MTSP), and the minimization of discontinuities problem (MDP). We deal with these questions, and,\nmoreover, we show that the MOSP is deeply related to a problem in the :eld of VLSI layout, thus\nenabling a link between researchers in computer science and operations research. ? 2002 Published by\nElsevier Science Ltd.\n\nKeywords: Pattern sequencing; Flexible machines; VLSI layout; Computational complexity\n\n\n\n    ∗\n    Corresponding author. Brazilian School of Public Administration, EBAP=FGV, Praia Botafogo 190=426, Rio de\nJaneiro 22257-970, Brazil.\n  E-mail addresses: linhares@fgv.br (A. Linhares), horacio@lac.inpe.br (H.H. Yanasse).\n\n0305-0548/02/$ - see front matter ? 2002 Published by Elsevier Science Ltd.\nPII: S 0 3 0 5 - 0 5 4 8 ( 0 1 ) 0 0 0 5 4 - 5\n\f1760           A. Linhares, H.H. Yanasse / Computers & Operations Research 29 (2002) 1759–1772\n\n1. Introduction\n\n   Consider a production setting where J distinct patterns need to be cut. Each one of these\npatterns may contain a combination of at most I piece types. We can de:ne the piece-pattern\nrelationship by a I × J binary matrix P = {pij }, with pij = 1 if pattern j contains piece type i,\nand pij = 0 otherwise.\n   When a pattern is cut, the pieces are stored on stacks that remain :xed around the cutting\nsaw until each stack is completed. Each stack holds only pieces of the same type and it re-\nmains open until the last pattern containing that piece type is cut. DiIculties in handling a\nlarger number of distinct open stacks appear, for instance, if the number increases beyond the\nsystem capability, so some of the stacks must be temporarily removed, yielding additional costs,\nhigher production time, and higher associated risks (as observed in glass-cutting settings). We\nare thus interested in the sequencing of the patterns to minimize the maximum number of open\nstacks during the cutting process (An analogous problem arises from the sequencing of tasks in\na *exible machine with tooling constraints [1]). An open stacks versus cutting instants matrix\nQ = {qij } is de:ned by\n               \u0001\n                   1; if ∃x; ∃y | (x) 6 j 6 (y) and       pix = piy = 1;\n       qij =                                                                                     (1)\n                   0; otherwise;\n\nwhere denotes a permutation of the {1; 2; : : : ; J } numbers, and de:nes a sequence on which\nthe patterns are cut, such that (i) is the order (instant) in which the ith pattern is cut. Note\nthat matrix Q holds the consecutive-ones property for columns [2] under permutation : in\neach row, any zero between two ones will be changed to one. This is called a :ll-in. Since we\nare interested in minimizing the number of simultaneous open stacks, we de:ne the following\ncost functional:\n\n                                         I\n                                         \u0002\n       ZMOSP (P) = max                         qij                                               (2)\n                       j∈{1; ::: ; J }\n                                         i=1\n\n\nand de:ne the minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) as the problem of min ∈\u0010 ZMOSP (P),\nwhere \u0010 is the set of all possible one-to-one mappings : {1; 2; : : : ; J } → {1; 2; : : : ; J }.\n   In Yanasse [1], a nontrivial mathematical formulation is presented, a branch and bound scheme\nto solve it is proposed, and a number of conjectures are raised. In this work we deal with those\nconjectures. Section 2 deals with the computational complexity of the MOSP, and the relations\nto problems such as modi:ed cutwidth and problems in VLSI design. Section 3 considers\nsome related pattern-sequencing problems, focusing on the possibility of solving a speci:c\nproblem by approaching a related one. Section 4 places a new conjecture, based on a previously\nunknown relation between the minimization of tool switches problem and the minimization of\ndiscontinuities problem, which will be de:ned below. The conclusion discusses how our results\nin*uence the development of real applications. The :rst conjecture that should be dealt with\nconcerns the computational complexity of the MOSP: is MOSP NP-hard?","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
