ver, though the algorithms for minor recognition have low degree\npolynomials as worst case bounds, their constants of proportionality are enormous,\nrendering them impractical for practical problems (see e.g. [J087a]). The general\nbound is O(n3) for a graph with n vertices, and even O(n2) if the family F excludes\na planar graph. This is the case for the path width (see [RS83] and Proposition 3.12\nbelow), and thus:\n3.8 Theorem: Within the class of graphs with bounded pathwidth k, k fixed, the interval\ngraph augmentation problem can be solved in O(n2) time for a graph with n vertices.\nThis result is interesting in view of the NP-hardness of the general problem when\nk is part of the input (see S'!ction 4). A polynomial dynamic optimization algorithm\nfor fixed k of order O(n 2k2 +4 k+8) has also been obtained in the context of graph","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
