{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VTazvptCyDV9N79rz4Xhr7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tfor fixed k of order O(n 2k2 +4 k+8) has also been obtained in the context of graph\n2\tsearching [EST87] (see also below). It is, however, open how to design a practical\n3\tO(n2) algorithm.\n4\tThe application ot the Robertson-Seymour theory to gate matrix layout is discussed\n5\t(among other problems) in a series of papers [FL87] [FL88a] [FL88b]. For a\n6\tsurvey about computational implications of the Robertson-Seymour theory, we\n7\trefer to [J087a].\n8\tNode searching. This problem formulation refers to a searching game on graphs\n9\tintroduced in [KP86] as a variant of the more investigated edge searching [Pa76].\n10\tIn node searching, the edges of a graph represent a system of pipes or tunnels that\n11\tare considered contaminated by a gas. The object of node searching is to clear all\n12\tedges by a search. A search is a sequence of moves where a player places a searcher\n13\t\f34                                   Rolf H. Mohring\n14\t(also called guard) on a node of the graph that carries no searcher or deletes the\n15\tsearcher from a guarded node.\n16\tAn edge is cleared if both its endpoints simultaneously carry a searcher. A cleared\n17\tedge may be recontaminated if, at a later stage of the search, there is a path from an\n18\tuncleared edge to the cleared edge without any searchers on it. So in order to avoid\n19\trecontamination of cleared edges, the guarded nodes must after each move form a\n20\tseparating set that separates the still unsearched part of the graph (the not yet visited\n21\tvertices) from the already searched part (all vertices that carried a searcher in the\n22\tpast).\n23\tA search is called optimal if the maximum number of searchers on the graph at any\n24\tpoint is as small as possible. This number is called the node-search number of G, and\n25\tdenoted by ns(G).\n26\tIt was shown in [KP86] that there always is an optimal search without recontami-\n27\tnation of cleared edges. This was used in [KP85] to show the following unexpected\n28\trelationships to interval graph augmentation:\n29\t3.9 Theorem: For any graph G, ns(G) = t(G).\n30\tThe proof of this theorem is based on the following ideas. If H is an interval graph\n31\taugmentation of G, then any consecutive arrangement C1 , ••• , Ck of the maximum\n32\tcliques of H defines a search by letting the searchers move in this order through\n33\tC1 , ••• , Ck • If Ci is guarded, then searchers from C; - Ci+1 and possibly new search-\n34\ters may be moved to Ci+1 - C; until Ci+1 is guarded. It is easy to see that this\n35\tdefines a search without recontamination with w(H) searchers.\n36\tIn the converse direction, any recontamination-free search of G assigns to every\n37\tnode v of G the interval [i,j] whose endpoints are the first and last step in the search\n38\tat which v is occupied by a searcher. (Note that this definition makes sense since\n39\tthe search is recontamination-free.) Since every edge is searched, the intervals\n40\tassigned to its endpoints intersect. So the interval repre<!entation induces an interval\n41\tgraph H with E(G)!;; E(H). The maximum number k of searchers in this search\n42\tis obviously just the maximum number of pairwise intersecting intervals, i.e.\n43\tw(H).\n44\tNode searching is also closely related to other linear graph layout problems and to\n45\tpebbing games on graphs [KP86]. In particular, the interpretation as progressive\n46\tpebbling game gives the following useful result [KP86] for investigating or generat-\n47\ting searches.\n48\tConsider an acyclic orientation of the edges of G and a dynamic assignment of\n49\tsearchers to vertices that observes the rules\n50\t(3.8)       A vertex may accept a searcher only when all its immediate\n51\t            predecessors carry a searcher.\n52\t(3.9)             Every vertex is assigned a searcher exactly once.\n53\tThen:\n54\t\f              Graph Problems Related to Gate Matrix Layout and PLA Folding             35\n55\t                                             1   IN1              11N4\n56\t                                             2         1-1\n57\t                                                        N_2\n58\t                                             3               I   N3      II   N6   I\n59\t               G with acyclic                           The associated search in\n60\t               orientation                              interval representation\n61\t                            Figure 3.3. An illustration of a search\n62\t3.10 Proposition: Every assignment observing (3.8) and (3.9) defines a recontamination-\n63\tfree search of G, and every recontamination-free search of G can be obtained in that\n64\tway.\n65\tThe first part follows easily by induction and the observation that the ''foremost''\n66\tsearchers (those that have an unvisited immediate successor) form a separating set\n67\tin G that separates the searched part from the unsearched part. The other direction\n68\tis obtained by considering the orientation of G defined by u < v if u is visited by a\n69\tsearcher before v. An example is given in Figure 3.3.\n70\tWe call such a search a directed search.\n71\tAnother useful application is the combination of the search interpretation with a\n72\tstructural decomposition of graphs, the split decomposition [Cu82].\n73\tA split in an undirected graph G is a partition V(G) = VI U V2 of the vertex set of\n74\tG such that I\"II ;;:: 2 (i = 1,2) and\n75\t(3.10) The edges of of G going from VI to V2 induce a com!)lete bipartite graph.\n76\t3.11 Lemma: Let V(G) == VI U V2 be a split of G and let AI !;;; V; (i = 1,2) be the\n77\tvertices of the associated complete bipartite graph. Then every recontamination-free\n78\tsearch of G has a step at which all vertices from Al or all vertices from A2 simulta-\n79\tneously carry a searcher. So in particular, ns(G) ;;:: min{IAII, IA 2 1}.\n80\tThis can be seen as follows. If the statement is not true, then there is a first step of\n81\tthe search at which a searcher is deleted from the endpoint (u, say) of an already\n82\tcleared edge (u, v) E Al X A 2. Since neither Al nor A2 are completely visited at that\n83\tstep, there is an uncleared edge (x,y) E Al X A 2. But then (u,v) is recontaminated\n84\tvia the path (x, y), (y, u).\n85\tStill another application is the following argument from [KP86], which, in the\n86\tcontext of edge. searching, is due to [Pa76]. It also shows that the search number\n87\tis unbounded on the class of trees. This gives also the missing argument for the\n88\tO(n2) algorithm for bounded tree width (the class of graphs with bounded tree width\n89\texcludes some trees and thus a planar graph).\n90\t\f36                                   Rolf H. Mohring\n91\t3.12 Lemma: Let G contain a vertex v of degree 3 whose deletion separates G into\n92\tthree connected components G1 , G2 , G3 , each of which has node search number\n93\tns(G;) = t. Then ns(G) = t + 1.\n94\tIt is easy to see that t + 1 searchers suffice. (Put a searcher on v and search G1 , G2 ,\n95\tG3 with the remaining t searchers). To show that they are also necessary, assume\n96\tthat t searchers suffice for G.\n97\tSince t searchers are already required for each of the subgraphs G1 , G2 , G3 , there\n98\tis a moment at which one of them (G 1 , say) has already been searched, all searchers\n99\tare on the second one (G2 , say), and the last of them is still unvisited. But then\n100\trecontamination takes place between G3 and G1 , a contradiction;\n101\tAs a consequence, one obtains [KP86]:\n102\t3.13 Proposition: The search number of a complete ternary tree T is equal to its height\n103\tplus one.\n104\tIt was already mentioned that node searching is a variant of the more investigated\n105\tedge searching. In edge searching, an edge is cleared by letting a searcher go through\n106\tit (instead of by occupying both endpoints as in node searching). It is therefore\n107\tpossible [KP86] to obtain (optimal) node searchers on G from (optimal) edge\n108\tsearches on a slight modification of G (replace each edge of G by three parallel\n109\tedges). Exploitation of this transformation and known results for edge searching on\n110\ttrees [MHGJP88] and dynamic programming formulations [EST87] yield a linear\n111\ttime search algorithm for trees (see also Section 4) and a polynomial time algorithm\n112\tof order O(n 2k2 + 4 k+8) for graphs with search number at most k, k fixed.\n113\tAlternating paths. This final equivalence is considered in many papers on PLA\n114\tfolding. It views tracks in a layout of M as directed paths in the complement G of\n115\tthe incompatibility graph G of M. The directed edges of these paths may be\n116\tconsidered as being added to G. This gives the following formulation.\n117\tLet G be the incompatibility graph of a MPP, and consIder the edges of G as colored\n118\tred. A path partition (or multiple folding) of G is a set of directed green edges F such\n119\tthat the following two conditions are satisfied.\n120\t(3.11)   The subgraph defined by the green edges is a collection of directed paths\n121\t         (i.e., indegree and outdegree of every vertex is at most 1). This constraint is\n122\t         called the degree constraint.\n123\t(3.12)   There exists no cycle of alternating directed green path segments and red\n124\t         edges (alternating cycle). This constraint is called the cycle constraint.\n125\tThe size of a path partition is the number of green paths.\n126\t3.14 Theorem: Determining a minimum size path partition is polynomially equivalent\n127\tto interval graph augmentation.\n128\tThis can be seen as follows. From a path partition with t paths, one can construct\n129\tan interval representation of an interval augmentation H of G with w(H) = t by a\n130\tleft to right scan through the t paths. Initially, the intervals corresponding to the\n131\t\f               Graph Problems Related to Gate Matrix Layout and PLA Folding              37\n132\t                                                 N, ___       Ns ---        Ns\n133\t                                                 N2 - - N3 - - N6 - - N7\n134\t          Ns L -_ _ _ _              Ns\n135\t                      G                                 A path partition of G\n136\t                          I N, II Ns II Ns I\n137\t                          BJI N3 II N6    IE]\n138\t                          I N4               I\n139\t                            The associated interval representation\n140\t                            of Theorem 3.15\n141\t                          Figure 3.4. An illustration of a path partition\n142\tminimal vertices in the t paths are \"opened\". When an interval is closed, the interval\n143\tof the next vertex in the corresponding path is opened etc. Given a collection of t\n144\topened intervals, the next interval to close corresponds to a vertex u such that there\n145\tare no red edges (u, w) to a vertex w that occurs after a currently open interval v on\n146\ta green path containing v. Note that there is such a vertex u because of (3.12).\n147\tSince exactly t intervals are open at any moment, w(H) = t. To see that H augments\n148\tG, let (u, v) E E(G) and, w.l.o.g., let u be opened before v(as intervals). Then the choice\n149\tdefined above ensures that u is only closed after v is opened. Hence the correspond-\n150\ting intervals overlap. An example of this construction is given in Figure 3.4.\n151\tThe converse direction is obvious since every optimal coloring of an optimal interval\n152\tgraph augmentation H of G defines a path partition of size X(H) = w(H). This\n153\tinterpretation is particularly useful for the special cases of the MPP dealing with\n154\tPLA folding.\n155\tThere are several other equivalent or related graph theoretic notions. We just\n156\tmention vertex separation, min cut linear arrangement, bandwidth and several\n157\tmodifications of these problems. While vertex separation is equivalent to node\n158\tsearch [KP86], the other notions define bounds on t( G) [KP86] [B088].\n159\tRestrictions of the MPP. The restrictions discussed in Section 2 have natural\n160\tformulations within several of the different representations of this section.\n161\t\f38                                  Rolf H. Mohring\n162\tFor instance, the WMPP can be modeled by fixing two cliques of G as belonging\n163\tto the first and last maximal clique of the interval augmentation to construct, or\n164\tby requiring the searchers to start and finish their search on specified cliques of\n165\tG.\n166\tFor PLA folding problems, at most two vertices may share a common track. This\n167\tmeans in the path partition formulation that the directed green paths reduce to\n168\tdirected green edges. This gives the most common formulation of PLA-folding,\n169\twhich is due to [HNS82].\n170\t3.15 Proposition: Let G be the incompatibility graph of a P LA folding prob-\n171\tlem. Then the minimum number of tracks for a PLA folding is equal to IV(G)I - s,\n172\twhere s is the maximum number of green directed arcs that can be added to G such\n173\tthat\n174\t(3.13)   The green arcs form a matching in the complement ofG (degree constraint),\n175\t(3.14)   There is no alternating cycle of directed green edges and undirected red\n176\t         edges ofG (cycle constraint).\n177\tSuch a set F of green arcs is called a folding set or simply folding. So Proposition\n178\t3.15 states that the PLA-folding problem is equivalent to finding a folding set of\n179\tmaximum size.\n180\tAnother characterization can be obtained from the observation that, in an optimal\n181\tlayout of the associated augmented matrix M\", the rows can be permuted in such\n182\ta way that the rows with two nets appear on top and that the rightmost 1's of the\n183\tfirst nets in these rows define a \"decreasing staircase\".\n184\tThis staircase pattern corresponds to the special subgraph Zm.m of G defined below.\n185\tA Zm.m (also called a triangular clique in [HK87]) is a bipartite graph G = (U, V, E)\n186\twith U = {al, ... ,am }, V = {b 1 , ••• ,bm }, and E = {(ai' bj)1 1 i  m}.\n187\tThis gives [HK87]:\n188\t3.16 Proposition: Finding a maximum folding set in G is equivalent to finding a\n189\tmaximum size Zm,m as (partial, not induced) subgraph ofG.\n190\tBoth conditions can easily be sharpened for block folding and constrained folding\n191\tproblems. Call a folding set F resulting in a block folding a block folding set. Then\n192\tone obtains the following characterization of block folding, see e.g. [RL88].\n193\t3.17 Proposition: Let G be the incompatibility graph of a P LA folding problem. Then:\n194\t(1) A set F of directed green arcs added to G is a block folding set iff F satisfies the\n195\tdegree constraint (3.13) and\n196\t(3.15)   There is no red edge (u, v) from the head of green edge to the tail of another\n197\t         green edge.\n198\t(2) Finding a maximum block folding set in G is equivalent to finding a maximum size\n199\tK m • m (the complete bipartite graph on 2m vertices) as (partial, not induced) subgraph\n200\tofG.\n201\t\f              Graph Problems Related to Gate Matrix Layout and PLA Folding           39\n202\tRemember that in constrained PLA folding, the nets are preassigned to the two\n203\tsides of the layout. Thus only incompatibility relations between these two sides are\n204\tof importance, i.e., the incompatibility graph G can be assumed to be bipartite.\n205\tTherefore, constrained PLA-folding is sometimes also called bipartite folding\n206\t[EL84], [HK87].\n207\tThe above conditions then specialize further for block folding.\n208\t3.18 Proposition: Finding a maximum block folding in a bipartite graph G is equiva-\n209\tlent to finding a maximum size Km,m as induced subgraph of the (bipartite) complement\n210\tofG.\n211\t                               4. Complexity Results\n212\tIt was already mentioned several times that the general MPP is NP-hard. In fact,\n213\tthis has been obtained independently in many of the equivalent formulations, e.g.\n214\tfor interval graph augmentation in [KF79], for nQde search in [KP86], for directed\n215\tnode search in [Le82], and for path width in [ACP87].\n216\tThe NP-hardness of the PLA folding problems (including the general MPP, but\n217\texcluding block folding) is shown in [LVVS82] by a series of reductions from matrix\n218\tupper triangulation (given an n x n 0-1 matrix A, is there a permutation of the rows\n219\tand another of the columns such that resulting matrix is upper triangular?).\n220\tWe will here sketch a different series of reductions that starts from constrained block\n221\tfolding and contains block folding and a sharper version for interval graph aug-\n222\tmentation (i.e. the general MPP), which turns out to be NP-hard already on chordal\n223\tgraphs. Our starting point is (see e.g. [EL84]):\n224\t4.1 Theorem: Constrained block folding is N P-hard.\n225\tThis follows in fact directly from Proposition 3.19 that gi-:es the equivalence to\n226\t\"balanced complete bipartite subgraph\" which is stated to be NP-complete in\n227\t[GJ79] (the proof has appeared in [Jo87b]).\n228\tPreassignment of nets to sides can easily be enforced by adding two gates Go, Gn+1\n229\tthat are connected to the nets from the left and right side, respectively. Then any\n230\t(block) folding of the augmented problem can only fold nets incident to Go with\n231\tnets incident to Gn+1 , and the sides are (up to reversal of the layout) fixed by the\n232\tposition of Go and Gn + 1 • In view of Theorem 4.1, this gives:\n233\t4.2 Theorem: Block folding is N P-hard.\n234\tThis result has been sharpened in [MW89] by a different reduction from GRAPH\n235\tBISECTION. Exploiting techniques from [BCLS87], [WW89] they obtain:\n236\t4.3 Theorem: Block folding is N P-hard even for graphs with degree at most k for any\n237\tfixed k;;::: 3.\n238\tThe reduction from block folding to IGA on chordal graphs is based on the\n239\tfollowing equivalent formulation of block folding.\n240\t\f40                                     Rolf H. Mohring\n241\tGiven:       A graph G and an integer k.\n242\tQuestion: Is there a partition of V(G) into three sets Vi> V2 , V3 such that\n243\t          (i) every path from VI to V3 goes through a vertex of V2 ,\n244\t          (ii) min {lVII, 1V3 J} 2': k?\n245\tThe answer to such an instance is obviously yes iff there exists a block folding set\n246\tof G with k green edges (viz. from vertices to VI to vertices of V3). Based on this\n247\tformulation, the following result is obtained in [Gu89].\n248\t4.4 Theorem: Gate matrix layout and pathwidth are already NP-hard on the class of\n249\tchordal graphs.\n250\tRecall that a graph is chordal (or triangulated) if every elementary cycle VI' v 2 , ••. ,\n251\t                                                       v) with 1 ::;;; i < j + 1 ::;;; k + 1.\n252\tVk' VI oflength k 2': 4 possesses a chord, i.e. an edge (Vi'\n253\tChordal graphs form a natural generalization of interval graphs, see e.g. [G080]\n254\tfor more information about chordal graphs. The chordal graphs needed in the proof\n255\tare quite special. They consist of a set of maximal cliques that overlap in a central\n256\tclique.\n257\tIn more detail, let G, k be an instance of the above formulation of block folding\n258\tsuch that G is w.l.o.g. connected. From G we construct such a special chordal graph\n259\tH as follows. H contains a maximal clique Co (called the central clique) with vertex\n260\tset V(G). For each edge (u, v) E E(G), a maximal clique Cuv is added that consists of\n261\tthe vertices u, v E Co and IV(G)I additional vertices that are incident only to vertices\n262\tin Cuv • Clearly, this graph is of the desired type.\n263\tFrom H one can construct a net-gate matrix M with incompatibility graph H by\n264\tintroducing a gate for each of the maximal cliques of H. Consider an augmented\n265\tmatrix M\" of M and let C+ and C- be the cliques (gates) before and after the central\n266\tclique (gate) Co in M\". Since every net corresponding to an original vertex of G that\n267\tis incident to some gate from C+ or C- is also incident to Co, the order of the gates\n268\tin C+ or C- does not influence the number of tracks.\n269\tLet V+ and V- denote the nets of Co (vertices of G) incident to a gate from C+\n270\tand C- , respectively, and let VI := V+ - V-, V2 := V+ n V- and V3 := V- - V+.\n271\tIt can then be shown that VI' V2 , V3 form a partition of V(G) with the sbove\n272\tdisconnecting property, and that min {lVII, 1V31} 2': k is equivalent to t(M) ::;;; 2·\n273\tIV(G) I - k.\n274\tConversely, every partition VI' V2 , V3 of G with the above properties can be\n275\ttransformed into an augmented matrix M\" by first taking all gates (cliques) Cuv with\n276\tu, v E VI U V2 (in any order), followed by Co and all cliques Cuv with u, v E V2 U V3.\n277\tThis proves the theorem. An example of this construction is given in Figure 4.1.\n278\tNote that if all maximal cliques that intersect the central clique are mutually disjoint,\n279\tthen the problem can be solved in 0(1 V(GW) time by a dynamic programming\n280\talgorithm [Gu89]. This confirms that the borderline between easy and hard pro-\n281\tblems for subclasses of chordal graphs G depends essentially on the overlapping\n282\tbehavior of the maximal cliques of G (see also [ACP87]).\n283\t\f                Graph Problems Related to Gate Matrix Layout and PLA Folding          41\n284\t                .0:\n285\t                                                  v, = {1}\n286\t                                                  V2 = {2,4}\n287\t                                                  V3 = {3}\n288\t                         G\n289\t                                               An associated partition\n290\t          