 St.Petersburg, 198904, Russia,\n                      e-mail: Fomin@gamma.niimm.spb.su\nAbstract\n   We suggest a uniform game-theoretic approach to \\width\" graph parameters. We\nconsider a search problem on a graph in which one cop in a helicopter ying from\nvertex to vertex tries to catch the robber. The existence of the winning program\nfor the cop in this problem depends only on the robber's speed. We investigate the\nproblem of nding the minimal robber's speed which prevents the cop from winning.\nWe examine two cases of the problem. In the rst one the cop can visit each vertex\nof a graph only once. In the second case the cop cannot a ord \\recontamination\" of\nvertices. We show that in the rst case the problem of nding the minimal robber's\nspeed is equivalent to the bandwidth minimization problem. In the second case we\nshow that the problem is equivalent to the natural generalization of the bandwidth\nproblem and is closely approximated by the pathwidth. Also we show that the\nproblem of computing the minimal robber's speed is NP-hard in both cases.\nKeywords: Searching a graph, bandwidth, pathwidth, computational complexity.\n1 Introduction\nMany pursuit-evasion processes on graphs have been analyzed in the last\ntwenty years. Often a pursuit-evasion process is described as a two-person\ngame. In some games pursuers have complete information on actions of evader,\nsee [1{3] for further references, in some games no information at all. In the\nlatter case we deal with a search problem which was considered in various\nformalizations by many authors in [6,9,11,13,24,25], see also surveys [4,10]. In\nthis paper we consider the following problem of \\guaranteed\" search.\nWe use the word graph to denote a nite undirected topological graph, that\nis embedded in a Euclidean space (dimension of this space is not important\nfor us). We shall assume in this paper that edges of a graph are one unit long.\nAlso we consider only connected graphs with at least two vertices.\nPreprint submitted to Elsevier Science\n\fLet a pursuit-evasion game be played on a graph with the vertex set V\nand the edge set E . Two players called Cop and Robber are on . Cop tries\nto nd Robber, and Robber tries to avoid capture. Cop's actions are de ned\nby a nite sequence of steps called search program \u0005. In the rst step, Cop\noccupies some vertex of . In each of the following steps, Cop moves ( ies by\nhelicopter) to some vertex (not necessarily adjacent to the occupied vertex)\nof . So the search program \u0005 is a mapping\n                            \u0005: f1; 2; : : :; T g ! V ;\nwhere \u0005(i), i 2 f1; : : : ; T g, is the vertex occupied by Cop in the i-th step.\nA continuous mapping\n                                 y: [0; T ] !\nis interpreted as a trajectory of Robber. We shall suppose that the Robber's\nspeed is restricted by the constant \u0016, i.e. for any t1; t2 2 [0; T ], t1 6= t2,\n                               \u001a(y(t1); y(t2)) \u0014 \u0016;\n                                  t1 t2\nwhere \u001a(y(t1); y(t2)) is the length (in the Euclidean metric) of the shortest\ncurve in that connects y(t1), y(t2). Thus Robber cannot leave , and can\novercome a distance of no more than \u0016 with every step of Cop.\nCop nds Robber in the i-th step if and only if \u001a(\u0005(i); y(i)) < 1. If edges\nof the graph are segments, then Cop positioned in any vertex \\oversees\" all\nincident edges. In this case we deal with a problem of the \\saw-caught\" type.\nThe problems of this kind were considered in [8,26,28]. The search program\n\u0005(i), i 2 f1; : : : ; T g, is a winning one if for any trajectory of Robber y(t),\nt 2 [0; T ], there exists i 2 f1; : : : ; T g, such that by the i-th step Robber is\nfound.\nNote that this search problem can be interpreted as a problem of clearing\nthe graph from \\di used\" Robber. We say that in program \u0005 point x 2 is\ncontaminated at a moment t\u0003 \u0015 1, if there exists a trajectory y(t), t 2 [0; t\u0003],\nsuch that y(t\u0003) = x, and for every i 2 f1; : : :; bt\u0003cg, \u001a(y(i); \u0005(i)) \u0015 1. Let us\ndenote by CONT (\u0005; ; t\u0003) the set of points of graph contaminated at the\nmoment t\u0003. We shall suppose that for all t 2 [0; 1), = CONT (\u0005; ; t). Taking\nthis assumption into account, we can state that program \u0005(i), i 2 f1; : : : ; T g,\nis a winning one if and only if for some i\u0003 2 f1; : : :; T g, CONT (\u0005; ; i\u0003) = ;.\nThe existence of the winning program for Cop in this problem depends only\non the constant \u0016. Let us consider for a graph the parameter \u0016( ), which\nis de ned as\n              inf f\u0016: with \u0016 Cop has no winning program on g:\n                                        2\n\fThe problem of computing \u0016( ) we call the helicopter search problem. In this\npaper we would like to point out a connection between the helicopter search\nproblem and a seemingly unrelated problems. To do this, we consider two\ncases of the search problem. In the rst case (section 2) we allow Cop to visit\neach vertex of a graph only once. In the second one (section 3) Cop can visit\nvertices more than once but contamination of previously visited vertices is not\nallowed.\nIn further arguments we shall use the following simple but important for us\nfact.\nLemma 1 Let \u0005(i), i 2 f1; : : : ; T g, be a search program on a graph , and\nlet A be a vertex of . If there exists a moment t\u0003 2 [0; T ] such that A 2\nCONT (\u0005; ; t\u0003) and for all i 2 fdt\u0003e; : : : ; T g, \u0005(i) 6= A, then \u0005 is not a\nwinning one.\n2 Bandwidth\nIn this section we discuss the connection between the helicopter search problem\nand the bandwidth minimization problem. The problem of determining the\nbandwidth of a graph arises in di erent elds of mathematics. See [7] for a\ndiscussion of bandwidth and its applications.\nA linear layout of a graph is a one-to-one mapping\n                          L: