{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZWBcuQAvfPfx8mLk3h2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 01, the statement census, is done. `paper2/equivalences.md` now covers all twelve Table 1 problems: each has the source's own definition quoted with page and number, what it is defined on, the graph it maps to, the exact relation to pathwidth with its theorem number, and a verdict. The gate passes (lake build ok, 1 sorry against a limit of 1, 1,217 tests pass). No Lean or checker code was written, as the item required.\n\nI read Table 1's \"±1\" two ways: as the literal claim that a value lies within one of the open-stack count Z, and as the stronger claim of a fixed offset.\n\n**Verdicts:**\n- **Confirmed (exact):**\n  - **MOSP:** the cited [1] and [4] only define the problem. The relation to pathwidth comes from Yanasse 1997a, Fellows & Langston 1989 and Linhares & Yanasse Prop. 2, and is already proved in Lean.\n  - **Gate matrix layout:** equal to MOSP on the same matrix, with no transposition (rows are nets, which play the part of piece types).\n  - **One-dimensional logic:** confirmed for the core problem only. The variant with boundary gates fixed at the ends is not within ±1.\n  - **Narrowness:** narrowness = pathwidth + 1.\n  - **Node search:** 1985 proves node search = interval thickness, and 1986 proves node search = vertex separation + 1. Both fail on edgeless graphs, where the node search number is 0.\n  - **Vertex separation:** vertex separation = pathwidth.\n- **Unsourced at its citation:** interval thickness. The cited paper [5] is still not held, but Möhring Prop. 3.5 proves the relation.\n- **Weaker than stated:**\n  - **Edge search** is a band: vertex separation ≤ edge search ≤ vertex separation + 2, and all three values occur.\n  - **Split bandwidth** is a sandwich: pathwidth ≤ split bandwidth ≤ pathwidth + 1, and both values occur.\n  - Both still meet the literal \"±1 of open stacks\" wording.\n- **False as stated:**\n  - **PLA folding:** in Möhring's own sense (at most two nets per track) it is not within ±1. The 5×5 identity matrix needs 3 tracks against 1. Multiple folding is exact (his Thm 3.14).\n  - **Edge separation:** misattributed. Lengauer's edge game is min-cut linear arrangement (cutwidth), and his Definition 6 is modified cutwidth. Both have unbounded gap to pathwidth on stars. His vertex game is exactly vertex separation, which is already Table 1's last row.\n\nThe star values (cutwidth of K₁,₇ = 4, modified cutwidth of K₁,₉ = 4) were checked with a throwaway script. The other counterexamples are hand arguments, listed for item 02 to verify: the identity matrix, the boundary-gate family, and split bandwidth of K₁,₃ = 2.\n\n**Other findings:**\n- Table 1 is on p. 1764 of the 2002 paper, not p. 1762 as `MANIFEST.md` says. I left `MANIFEST.md` alone because it is outside this item.\n- Fink & Voss transpose the MOSP matrix (their rows are patterns).\n- Wing et al.'s full problem adds a realizability constraint that Table 1's version drops.\n\nThe file ends with eleven statements for item 02's brute-force checker to test. One design question is still open: split bandwidth needs a bound on the number of node splittings before it can be brute-forced.\n\nPROGRESS.md now reads 1/14 SOLVED with the iteration section, and item 01 is marked `[x]` in `iterations.md`. Nothing was committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1653,"cache_read_input_tokens":229917,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1653,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":776,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9ade16e4-7c1a-4abd-aec7-7c767f55a728","uuid":"b9bbeec3-fc79-4229-ae5c-7262294d2309","timestamp":"2026-09-30T10:57:21.65